WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.194
In the 1950s, Taiwan's countryside began to change.

00:00:04.194 --> 00:00:07.609
Many farmers rented land to farm, and life was not easy.

00:00:07.609 --> 00:00:11.499
Water buffalo in the fields worked alongside farmers from sunrise.

00:00:11.499 --> 00:00:14.842
The government introduced the 37.5% rent reduction policy.

00:00:14.842 --> 00:00:18.393
From then on, the rent farmers paid landlords was reduced.

00:00:18.393 --> 00:00:22.453
Villagers lined up to hear the news.

00:00:22.453 --> 00:00:25.648
Next came the sale of public land, so farmers could buy land.

00:00:25.648 --> 00:00:28.885
Contract after contract changed relationships in the countryside.

00:00:28.885 --> 00:00:33.431
'Land to the tiller' let farmers own their own land.

00:00:33.431 --> 00:00:36.752
Farmers stood by their fields, beaming with joy.

00:00:36.752 --> 00:00:40.072
Owning land gave farmers new motivation.

00:00:40.072 --> 00:00:43.979
Villages slowly grew wealthier, and children could go to school.

00:00:43.979 --> 00:00:48.141
Farmers worked hard, and harvests improved year after year.

00:00:48.141 --> 00:00:51.280
New houses were built in the villages, and schools were opened.

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:54.997
Land reform shaped later economic development.

00:00:54.997 --> 00:00:58.680
It laid a stable foundation for Taiwan's society.
